# Userscripts

A subset of features is also published as standalone Tampermonkey userscripts. An entry under
`src/userscripts/entries/` is a **thin bootstrap**: it wires up userscript implementations of the runtime singletons and
then registers the very same `Feature` class the extension uses. Feature logic belongs in `src/common/features/`, never
duplicated into the entry.

## Bump the version when you change shared code

If you change anything under `src/common/features/X/` and `src/userscripts/entries/X/` exists, bump `version` in that
entry's `metadata.ts`.

`.github/workflows/userscripts.yml` triggers **only** on changes to `src/userscripts/entries/**/metadata.ts`. Without
the bump your fix builds fine locally and is never published. Bump patch for fixes and behaviour changes, minor for new
capability. Versions are per-script and independent of the extension version.

Entry folder names usually match the feature folder but not always — check the import in the `.user.ts` file rather than
assuming (`travel-cooldown` → `@features/travel-cooldowns/travel-cooldowns`). Only bump the scripts you actually
affected; `bun tools/bump-userscript-versions.ts` bumps all of them and is for release chores.

## Entry layout

```
src/userscripts/entries/my-script/
├── metadata.ts        # default-exported UserscriptMetadata
└── my-script.user.ts  # bootstrap
```

```ts
// metadata.ts
import type { UserscriptMetadata } from "@userscripts/entries/userscript-metadata";

const metadata: UserscriptMetadata = {
	name: "Shop Profits",
	description: "Display the amount of profit per city shop item.",
	version: "1.0.9",
	matches: ["https://*.torn.com/shops.php*"],
	runAt: "document-end",
	connect: ["torntools.tornplayground.eu"],
};

export default metadata;
```

`name`, `description`, `version`, `matches` and `runAt` are required. `connect` is required for **every** external
domain the script talks to via `GM.xmlHttpRequest` — a missing entry fails at runtime, not at build time. Author,
licence, icon, namespace and support URLs are added by the build script; don't put them in `metadata.ts`.

## Bootstrap and context tiers

Register only the tiers the feature actually needs, in this order, then register the feature:

```ts
import { FEATURE_MANAGER } from "@common/utils/context";
import ShopProfitsFeature from "@features/shop-profits/shop-profits";
import { registerCoreUserscriptContext } from "@userscripts/runtime/context/script-core-context";
import { registerDatabaseUserscriptContext } from "@userscripts/runtime/context/script-database-context";
import { registerNetworkUserscriptContext } from "@userscripts/runtime/context/script-network-context";
import { ScriptItemResolver } from "@userscripts/runtime/script-item-resolver";

(async () => {
	registerCoreUserscriptContext();
	await registerDatabaseUserscriptContext("tt_sp");
	registerNetworkUserscriptContext();
	await ScriptItemResolver.loadItems();

	FEATURE_MANAGER.registerFeature(new ShopProfitsFeature());
})();
```

| Tier | Register when |
| --- | --- |
| `registerCoreUserscriptContext()` | Always. Global CSS, feature manager, events, `unsafeWindow`, theme. |
| `await registerDatabaseUserscriptContext("tt_xx")` | The feature reads `settings`, `filters`, or any persisted state. |
| `registerNetworkUserscriptContext()` | The feature makes HTTP requests or uses `ITEM_RESOLVER`. |
| `await ScriptItemResolver.loadItems()` | The feature needs the item map. |
| `registerInjectorUserscriptContext()` | The feature hooks page fetch/XHR or reads `torn.model`. |
| `await requiresAPIKey()` | The feature calls the Torn API. Prompts the user and caches the key. |
| `setup<Page>Page()` from `@common/pages/` | Mirror whatever `script-manager.ts` calls for that page. |

The storage prefix passed to `registerDatabaseUserscriptContext` must be unique per script (`tt_sp`, `tt_jf`, `tt_ahf`).
Storage is **not** shared between installed userscripts.

## Behavioural differences from the extension

Keep these in mind when editing shared feature code, because the userscript path is easy to break from
`src/common/features/`:

- `ScriptFeatureManager` calls `initialise()` and `execute()` unconditionally. It never consults `isEnabled()`,
  `precondition()` or `requirements()`, and `reload()` is never called. Don't rely on those hooks for correctness that
  the userscript also needs.
- Cross-origin requests go through `GM.xmlHttpRequest`; `OFFLOAD_SERVICE` rejects. `fetchData` handles this, so use it
  rather than `fetch` directly.
- Database migrations are skipped under userscripts.
- Use `RUNTIME_INFORMATION.getWindow()` instead of `window`, since page globals need `unsafeWindow`.

When behaviour must genuinely differ, branch on `RUNTIME_INFORMATION.isUserscript()` inside the shared feature rather
than forking the logic into the entry:

```ts
enabled: () => FEATURE_MANAGER.isEnabled(TravelItemProfitsFeature) && !RUNTIME_INFORMATION.isUserscript(),
```

`isPDA()` from `@userscripts/utils/script-utils` detects Torn PDA, which uses `PDA_storage` and its own reload path.

## Building

```bash
bun run build:userscripts              # all entries → .output/userscripts/
bun run build:userscripts my-script    # one entry
bun run build:userscripts --beta       # beta namespace + version suffix
```

`@grant` lines are generated automatically from the bundle, so never write them by hand. Build the affected entry after
touching shared feature code — a userscript can break on missing context even when the extension build is green.
